[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: An enforced denial rejects every word starting with it, and only the exact word was measured

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-ENFORCED-DENIAL-IS-A-PREFIX
TITLE: An enforced denial rejects every word starting with it, and only the exact word was measured
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
patternType's addCheck branch rejects a value whose PREFIX is a denied word --
`builtins.match "(${d}).*"` -- so enforcing RELATIONS also rejects a field
titled RELATIONSHIP or RELATIONS_2.

That is the faithful transcription. `(?!^RELATIONS)` denies a prefix too: the
caret constrains where the lookahead applies, not how much of the input it
covers, so upstream rejects exactly the same set at a line start. RequirementType
already relies on the same reading, which is why DOCUMENT_FROM_FILE is reserved
along with DOCUMENT.

What was MEASURED is narrower than what is enforced. The defect behind the
enforcement was measured on the exact word RELATIONS: a grammar declaring that
field exports clean and its documents then fail to parse. Nobody has run the
same experiment on RELATIONSHIP, so it is possible -- not likely, but untested
-- that the document grammar recovers on a longer word and the type is stricter
than the parser.

Fix shape: render a grammar declaring a RELATIONSHIP field, export it, and read
a document that uses it. If it parses, the two denial kinds want different
enforcement -- a prefix test for the reserved-keyword kind and an equality test
for this one -- and patternType needs the second branch.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed while wiring the enforcement: `deny` was reused because patternType
already carried the branch, and reuse imported prefix semantics along with it.
That is the right default, but it widens a rejection past the measurement that
justified it, and the widening is invisible in the generated file.
<<<
